WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:08.240
Visualizations are more than just data or charts&nbsp;
or maps, at their best they're a story. Humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.320
really have been telling each other stories&nbsp;
as their primary means of communications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:18.560
and education for tens of thousands of years.&nbsp;
Whether around a campfire or using modern media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.400
the fact of the matter is that there's a&nbsp;
lot of power to the phrase once upon a time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.880
We're wired for stories. And visualization&nbsp;
takes work to create stories out of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.160
and some of that work really has&nbsp;
nothing to do with the visualization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.880
It really is about the storytelling aspect to&nbsp;
it. Just like great design is about strategy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.840
thinking and planning before the first stroke of&nbsp;
the paintbrush, it's no less so in visualization.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.160
Visualizations, especially interactive ones,&nbsp;
aren't necessarily consumed in a linear way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.080
like a book. You can't necessarily control how&nbsp;
your user processes the information you give them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.520
but you can structure a story in a&nbsp;
narrative way with a narrative process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.840
You could encourage users to walk&nbsp;
through the information in a linear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.480
progressive way even though&nbsp;
you can't force them to do it.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.920
So, let's look at stories. The fact is that&nbsp;
stories have a very simple structure. Everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.360
has a beginning, a middle, and an end. A more&nbsp;
nuanced view includes a few more elements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.320
The beginning of the story and then the&nbsp;
protagonist faces a serious challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.840
and then there's the middle of the story&nbsp;
and then there's some sort of climax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.720
Then there's the denouement, that's&nbsp;
sort of like, after the climax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.080
things start to wrap up all the way down into&nbsp;
the grand finale where the book closes. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.480
think about your visualization in these terms&nbsp;
as much as you can, you really can't go wrong.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.680
Here, we're going to design a visualization&nbsp;
together using this structure as our guiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:53.200
principle. So, the first thing you do is you&nbsp;
define a headline, and there may be more than one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.880
headline. In fact, it is recommended that you go&nbsp;
through the process of thinking of more than one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.800
It is as if coming up with a New York Times&nbsp;
headline, which is a very serious and journalistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.240
and straightforward headline. And then there's the&nbsp;
New York Post version, which is a little bit more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:17.280
salacious and crazy and scandal-ridden. Sort of&nbsp;
the fun one versus the serious one. And sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:23.120
for your visualizations, you may lean one way or&nbsp;
the other, but play around with headlines. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.720
headline is the most important top line summation&nbsp;
of the story you're trying to tell. You can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.360
think about the headline in terms of, what will&nbsp;
people tweet when they share your visualization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.120
And you also want to, when you're coming up with&nbsp;
your headline, actually use blank spaces when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.480
you're thinking about it for the data parts.&nbsp;
So, in other words, you want to minimize bias.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.960
Your next point in the hierarchy of your data&nbsp;
visualization is your introduction. In an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.800
infographic like this, it's just a paragraph.&nbsp;
It's establishing the premise and the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.600
for the visualization before diving into the&nbsp;
data, and it belongs below the headline at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:07.120
top of the page to draw the eye there first.&nbsp;
Again, this is conceptual and structural,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:13.200
using the story as our guiding principle. This&nbsp;
is not meant to be the design. If you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.840
of it in the story standpoint the headline and&nbsp;
the introduction is the beginning of our story.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.280
Now let's move on to the challenge. There&nbsp;
are a lot of ways to add the “challenge”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.520
to a visualization. One is simply to add a&nbsp;
callout, big, bolded text like this that calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.440
out the main idea of the story you're trying&nbsp;
to tell or the problem that's being addressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.160
or the question that your visualization&nbsp;
is meant to answer. In this wire frame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:44.160
this bold type will really draw the eye. In a real&nbsp;
graphic, it could take different visual forms.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.600
We've set up the premise, we've gotten the user&nbsp;
into it, now we're going to load them up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.000
data, and here's where the chart with all the&nbsp;
detail and the interactivity and imagery is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.280
going to go, in this gray area in the middle.&nbsp;
This is where the user is going to spend most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.640
of their time really exploring and playing&nbsp;
around and looking at the data. Next, how do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.000
we introduce the climax, the sort of pivotal&nbsp;
moment in the experience? And we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.120
look at two different scenarios, one is a static&nbsp;
infographic and one is an interactive chart.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.200
In a static infographic, you have information&nbsp;
here, that's really meant to be consumed linearly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.080
Of course, people are going to start on the&nbsp;
left if they're left-to-right language readers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.520
They're going to follow the arrows, and more&nbsp;
likely than not, end up on the right-hand side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.600
But if we had a climax, a big story, a special&nbsp;
thing that we wanted to draw attention to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:43.360
we’re going to highlight it, maybe in a visually&nbsp;
interesting way. So, we’re going to create a focal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.880
point that's going to contain the climax. It could&nbsp;
actually contain the conclusion or the challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.080
This might draw the eye there first.&nbsp;
Of course , we might have killed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.600
linear progression experience because&nbsp;
someone is going to read this first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.440
But again, the idea isn't necessarily to&nbsp;
enforce linear consumption of the content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.920
it's just to use that structure&nbsp;
when you're thinking about it.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:11.680
Now, if we are trying to do this in an interactive&nbsp;
graphic, the approach might be a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:17.280
different. You can enforce a linear consumption&nbsp;
of the content by having a Next button and making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.920
your users click Next and Next to reveal one&nbsp;
piece of the story after another exactly how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.760
you want to tell it, and then you can do&nbsp;
the big reveal at the end of that process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.120
So, now we have the exact same visual&nbsp;
structure as the static infographic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.720
but we're forcing the user to get&nbsp;
to it piece by piece, bit by bit.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.720
Or another way to do it is to have&nbsp;
sorting and filtering buttons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:44.560
You might put your filters in a certain order,&nbsp;
and again, assuming people are going to go left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:50.640
to right, which is a relatively safe assumption,&nbsp;
but not always. You can encourage a user to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.040
certain filters first, clicking on the left,&nbsp;
and then when they click on that filter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:00.720
you might be able to highlight certain things.&nbsp;
When the data reveals, one bar is taller than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:04.480
others and you can use a callout to highlight&nbsp;
something interesting that happened there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:10.320
Again, the user is progressing through the&nbsp;
content, and there isn't necessarily a denouement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.040
Sometimes you might not get the opportunity&nbsp;
to conclude things for them because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.440
couldn’t find the things they&nbsp;
are looking for and just leave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.760
It's hard, in that situation, to offer conclusions&nbsp;
and to deconstruct things for them sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.120
but it's a really good exercise to try&nbsp;
to think about how you would do that.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:34.080
If we go back to our static infographic, it's&nbsp;
really easy to offer a conclusion on the page.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.360
We're used to seeing it in the lower right-hand&nbsp;
corner. You have a box with the small print,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:46.240
the footer. It's very easy to see structurally.&nbsp;
It is also strongly recommended that you always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:51.840
include your sources. Where did this data&nbsp;
come from? Why should one believe you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.040
Why is it valid? It is part of the&nbsp;
conclusion in a story structure.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:03.760
In an interactive infographic, you can also&nbsp;
offer more conclusion-like things. After your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.640
users go through this deep exploration process,&nbsp;
they're trying to find deeper information and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.840
their own discovery, they can deconstruct the info&nbsp;
themselves, but then at the end, there's a Next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:17.920
button and they click through and you can sort&nbsp;
of offer them this summed up view of the data.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:23.040
There are a lot of ways to introduce&nbsp;
conclusions and denouements into interactivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.000
even though you're never guaranteed&nbsp;
that people will experience them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.600
It's really helpful to think of every project,&nbsp;
even your non-linear interactive ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.360
within that linear storytelling framework. It&nbsp;
helps you work through functionality logic as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:43.520
well as the hierarchy of information, and&nbsp;
it also helps you measure your success.

